1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,536
This workshop connects five school reading rows to the same Week Two
patients. The detailed paired English and Chinese reading below
expands the chapter domains and every saved assigned BCPEG page,
rather than asking you to infer the lesson from a few headings.

2
00:00:16,536 --> 00:00:30,240
Chemistry explains concentrations and acid-base changes; autonomic
physiology explains receptor effects; pressure physiology and vital-
sign integration explain why the response must be checked in the
patient.

3
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:42,576
Use the reading index to open the book-specific bilingual guide after
the lecture. It includes definitions, mechanisms, clinical links,
source corrections and original retrieval tasks.

4
00:00:42,576 --> 00:00:59,040
The remaining school book rows, exact school exports and some
interactive content are still separate work, not declared complete by
this workshop. Source boundaries are visible so that the presence of a
reading button cannot be mistaken for full-week completion.

5
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:10,296
Moodle Week Two links five supplemental articles. Their original
English-Chinese guides are in the reading index; these slides do not
reproduce the articles or their cases.

6
00:01:10,296 --> 00:01:22,968
The overdose article teaches a respiratory emergency, but it dates
from 2013. Support ventilation first; use the current BCEHS pathway
for medication and follow-up decisions.

7
00:01:22,968 --> 00:01:35,520
The pain and nitrous articles prompt patient-centred assessment, self-
administration limits, non-drug relief and reassessment. They are not
local product instructions or permission to administer.

8
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:49,896
Bariatric airway and cough cases remind you to plan for reduced
reserve, positioning, ventilation, transport and competing causes. Do
not diagnose from one sign or import old dosing formulas.

9
00:01:49,896 --> 00:02:02,736
For any real intervention, use the current service guideline, exact
device instructions, licence level and training. Historical links are
discussion material, not a substitute for those controls.

10
00:02:02,736 --> 00:02:15,432
An amount tells how much substance is present. A concentration tells
how much of it is present in a specified amount of mixture or
solution. Neither number is useful until its units are named.

11
00:02:15,432 --> 00:02:29,064
For a fictional two milligrams in a final ten millilitres, the
concentration is zero point two milligrams per millilitre. Ten
millilitres is the final solution, not automatically the amount of
diluent added.

12
00:02:29,064 --> 00:02:41,016
An amount of one milligram would therefore require five millilitres.
The result still needs route, maximum, rate, patient and device checks
before any real administration.

13
00:02:41,016 --> 00:02:55,536
Chemistry also uses moles per litre, while Canadian glucose records
commonly use millimoles per litre. Do not turn a glucose concentration
into a drug volume by cancelling unlike substances or unrelated units.

14
00:02:55,536 --> 00:03:06,672
An element is identified by its number of protons. Change the neutron
number and you have an isotope; change the electron balance and you
have an ion, not a new element.

15
00:03:06,672 --> 00:03:20,928
Sodium and potassium ions help create electrical gradients across cell
membranes. An ECG records the combined electrical activity of heart
tissue; it is not a direct measurement of the blood potassium
concentration.

16
00:03:20,928 --> 00:03:31,584
Ionic charge is not a dose, and a radioisotope half-life is not a drug
elimination half-life. Similar-looking terms can describe different
physical processes.

17
00:03:31,584 --> 00:03:42,624
For the exam, be able to distinguish those terms before applying them.
A correct definition becomes useful when you can say what it does and
does not tell you about the patient.

18
00:03:42,624 --> 00:03:55,104
Sodium chloride separates into ions in water, while a glucose molecule
can dissolve without separating into sodium-like ions. Dissolving and
ionic dissociation are not the same statement.

19
00:03:55,104 --> 00:04:09,048
Polar water interacts well with many charged or polar groups. Nonpolar
regions behave differently, helping explain the organization of
phospholipid membranes and why formulation matters to drug delivery.

20
00:04:09,048 --> 00:04:21,768
Proteins have specific three-dimensional shapes and chemical binding
environments. A drug does not produce an effect merely by existing
somewhere in the body; it needs the appropriate target interaction.

21
00:04:21,768 --> 00:04:37,368
The detailed reading names all four organic groups and the relevant
structural vocabulary. Connect the molecular definition to a membrane,
enzyme, receptor or energy process instead of memorizing a
disconnected spelling list.

22
00:04:37,368 --> 00:04:52,512
A chemical reaction changes which atoms are joined and how the
products are arranged. Breaking bonds requires energy, while forming
bonds releases energy; the net reaction decides whether useful energy
is released.

23
00:04:52,512 --> 00:05:05,592
Enzymes lower activation energy and improve reaction rate under
suitable conditions. They do not create energy or erase the difference
between an energetically favourable and unfavourable reaction.

24
00:05:05,592 --> 00:05:19,008
ATP hydrolysis can be coupled to membrane pumps, muscle contraction
and synthesis. Think of the full reaction and the coupled work, not a
magical energy packet released by any bond breaking.

25
00:05:19,008 --> 00:05:32,664
This explains why oxygen delivery and substrate availability matter
but are not sufficient by themselves. The cells also need functioning
enzymes, membranes and circulation to sustain useful metabolism.

26
00:05:32,664 --> 00:05:45,360
Carbon dioxide is linked to water, carbonic acid, hydrogen ions and
bicarbonate through a reversible system. Removing carbon dioxide is
therefore part of maintaining acid-base balance.

27
00:05:45,360 --> 00:06:00,576
At otherwise comparable conditions, inadequate alveolar ventilation
retains carbon dioxide and tends toward respiratory acidosis. A normal
oxygen saturation on supplemental oxygen does not cancel that problem.

28
00:06:00,576 --> 00:06:11,328
Rapid breathing can instead be compensation for a metabolic acid load.
Labeling it anxiety and suppressing it without assessing the cause can
remove a useful compensation.

29
00:06:11,328 --> 00:06:26,256
The end-tidal value is influenced by ventilation, perfusion, dead
space and sampling. Use it with the patient and the complete
situation; do not announce a full acid-base diagnosis from one
displayed value.

30
00:06:26,256 --> 00:06:39,504
Glucose and glycogen are not interchangeable names: one is a
monosaccharide and the other a storage polymer. The available fuel and
its location matter when interpreting a glucose emergency.

31
00:06:39,504 --> 00:06:51,216
Lipids include energy-rich triglycerides and amphipathic phospholipids
that form membranes. Steroid precursors and signalling lipids have
roles beyond storing calories.

32
00:06:51,216 --> 00:07:05,304
Protein shape supports a specific function: an antibody, an enzyme and
a receptor do not perform the same task merely because they are all
proteins. Binding and denaturation depend on that structure.

33
00:07:05,304 --> 00:07:21,456
DNA and RNA are polymers made from nucleotides, whereas ATP is a
nucleotide used in energy coupling. Read the detailed section for
their components, then explain how a receptor protein and ATP-
dependent pump connect these categories.

34
00:07:21,456 --> 00:07:34,056
Autonomic motor pathways generally use a neuron from the central
nervous system to a ganglion, then a neuron from that ganglion to the
organ. Name both the transmitter and the receptor at each connection.

35
00:07:34,056 --> 00:07:48,888
Both divisions use acetylcholine at nicotinic ganglion receptors.
Parasympathetic organ targets generally use muscarinic receptors,
while most sympathetic organ targets receive norepinephrine at
adrenergic receptors.

36
00:07:48,888 --> 00:08:00,336
Eccrine sweat glands are an important sympathetic cholinergic
exception. This is why acetylcholine cannot simply be translated as
parasympathetic in every sentence.

37
00:08:00,336 --> 00:08:14,400
The adrenal medulla is a related endocrine branch that releases
catecholamines into blood. The diagram is a signal map, not a promise
that every autonomic action uses an identical anatomical distance or
circuit.

38
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:25,032
The same broad chemical family can increase cardiac activity,
constrict some vessels and relax bronchial smooth muscle because the
target receptors and their signalling differ.

39
00:08:25,032 --> 00:08:36,744
Use alpha one for the key vascular constrictor association, beta one
for important cardiac rate and force effects, and beta two for the
bronchodilator association relevant this week.

40
00:08:36,744 --> 00:08:47,856
Muscarinic effects also depend on the tissue and subtype. Reducing a
vagal effect on a node is not the same action as directly stimulating
every adrenergic receptor in the body.

41
00:08:47,856 --> 00:09:00,408
Receptor selectivity is not absolute isolation. A selected
bronchodilator can still cause tremor or tachycardia, so the expected
benefit and relevant harms must both be reassessed.

42
00:09:00,408 --> 00:09:15,240
After salbutamol, look for easier airflow, less work and improved
speech, while checking adverse cardiac effects and the actual inhaled
delivery. Do not measure success only by whether the inhaler was
emptied.

43
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:27,096
Ipratropium reduces bronchial muscarinic effects and is paired with
salbutamol under the indicated severe-bronchospasm plan. Their
mechanisms differ even when they are administered together.

44
00:09:27,096 --> 00:09:38,280
Epinephrine acts at several alpha and beta targets. That does not make
every route or advanced-care dose appropriate for a PCP anaphylaxis or
bronchospasm scenario.

45
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:51,600
Naloxone belongs on a different receptor map: it antagonizes opioid
effects. Its relevant endpoint is adequate ventilation, and it must
not distract the crew from opening and supporting the airway.

46
00:09:51,600 --> 00:10:03,480
A reflex has an input, integration and output, not merely a symptom.
Arterial stretch signals help brainstem circuits adjust autonomic
output to the heart and vessels.

47
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:17,232
When pressure or effective circulating volume falls, increased
sympathetic effects may help maintain perfusion. A beta blocker,
autonomic disease or severe deterioration can alter the expected pulse
response.

48
00:10:17,232 --> 00:10:29,088
Most systemic vessels do not have equal opposing parasympathetic
control. A lower sympathetic constrictor drive can produce dilation
without inventing a new parasympathetic impulse.

49
00:10:29,088 --> 00:10:45,120
Long reflexes involve the central nervous system; short enteric
reflexes can integrate locally. These distinctions help you understand
regulation, but a theoretical reflex diagram does not replace actual
blood pressure and perfusion checks.

50
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:57,408
Light input reaches a pretectal relay and bilateral parasympathetic
output through the Edinger-Westphal system. That bilateral connection
helps explain direct and consensual constriction.

51
00:10:57,408 --> 00:11:07,488
The efferent constrictor pathway travels with cranial nerve three
through the ciliary ganglion to the iris sphincter. Dilation uses a
different sympathetic pathway.

52
00:11:07,488 --> 00:11:18,552
The candidate chapter simplifies this into a hypothalamic relay. The
bilingual guide corrects that pathway without pretending that one
pupil sign can locate every lesion.

53
00:11:18,552 --> 00:11:32,952
Check pupil size, symmetry and reaction with the light conditions, eye
history, medication and the rest of the examination. Pinpoint pupils
support an opioid hypothesis but do not replace a respiratory
assessment.

54
00:11:32,952 --> 00:11:46,560
Pressure reflects an interaction between output and resistance, while
useful tissue delivery also depends on circulation and oxygen content.
A high cuff value alone cannot prove adequate forward flow.

55
00:11:46,560 --> 00:12:01,704
Primary hypertension is multifactorial. Secondary hypertension has an
underlying cause such as renal, endocrine, vascular or medication
disease; neither a young age nor one urine result decides the
distinction.

56
00:12:01,704 --> 00:12:15,720
Sustained high afterload can increase ventricular work and oxygen
demand, impair filling or ejection and contribute to heart failure.
Vascular injury can affect brain, kidneys, eyes and the aorta.

57
00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:28,152
Use that mechanism to understand the history and complications, not to
prescribe rapid pressure reduction from a textbook graph. The next
question is whether there is an acute dangerous syndrome now.

58
00:12:28,152 --> 00:12:40,776
Severe pressure with acute target-organ injury or an important acute
syndrome differs from an isolated asymptomatic reading. Both require
an appropriate plan, but not the same immediate treatment.

59
00:12:40,776 --> 00:12:54,384
Assess new neurologic findings, chest pain, severe dyspnea, awareness
and other relevant concerns. A high pressure can be part of the
problem, a response to it or a coincidental chronic condition.

60
00:12:54,384 --> 00:13:06,792
Verify measurement when safe and communicate the baseline, symptoms,
medicines and trend. Do not delay airway support while repeatedly
measuring a pressure that is already clinically concerning.

61
00:13:06,792 --> 00:13:20,424
Do not give extra home antihypertensives or try to make every number
normal rapidly. Chronic primary-care targets, acute stroke and
pregnancy emergencies follow different clinical pathways and
authority.

62
00:13:20,424 --> 00:13:35,304
The school focus names naloxone, salbutamol and epinephrine, but pages
sixty-six through seventy-seven include more. The bilingual reading
includes every named product in that actual page span, not only the
focus keywords.

63
00:13:35,304 --> 00:13:49,368
Use the same worksheet for each: class and mechanism, actual
indication, exclusions, amount and maximum, route and concentration,
timing, adverse effects and required reassessment.

64
00:13:49,368 --> 00:14:01,272
Then add prior doses and consultation or training conditions. A
correct amount taken from a page is not permission to use that
medicine for an unrelated symptom or by a different route.

65
00:14:01,272 --> 00:14:13,200
Some saved entries differ materially from current service guidance.
Learn the named examination statement and the current difference as
separate evidence, not a homemade hybrid regimen.

66
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:26,280
Examples include glucagon route and repeat consultation, the ibuprofen
pediatric fever schedule, the ondansetron age boundary and
nitroglycerin repeat requirements. These are not cosmetic differences.

67
00:14:26,280 --> 00:14:38,208
Record the source title, date and exact conflicting point. Never solve
the problem by taking the dose from one version, interval from another
and permission from a general textbook.

68
00:14:38,208 --> 00:14:52,704
Bring the conflict to the instructor before the official station or
exam, while using the actual current authorized pathway in clinical
practice. The package must not invent an answer to a school-version
question it cannot verify.

69
00:14:52,704 --> 00:15:05,112
The source panel and bilingual reading retain those differences. An
honest unresolved item is safer and more useful than an authoritative-
looking slide that silently changes its source.

70
00:15:05,112 --> 00:15:18,144
The Week 2 directed list assigns Anatomy and Physiology 2e section
6.5, page 815. We checked the exact course-folder PDF rather than
guessing from a different edition.

71
00:15:18,144 --> 00:15:39,744
In that course copy, section 6.5, “Fractures: Bone Repair,” is on
printed pages 212 to 214, physical PDF pages 228 to 230. The exact
page 815 is in section 20.1, “Structure and Function of Blood
Vessels.”

72
00:15:39,744 --> 00:15:57,312
The verified fracture section explains that fat globules released from
a femoral shaft fracture may travel to and lodge in pulmonary
capillary beds, causing respiratory distress. This is the assigned
clinical connection, but the school’s printed locator needs instructor
clarification.

73
00:15:57,312 --> 00:16:13,512
For the learner, the actionable lesson is to reassess the whole
patient and respond to new respiratory or neurologic deterioration
through the current pathway. Do not diagnose fat embolism from one
sign or treat this reading as a field protocol.

74
00:16:13,512 --> 00:16:25,608
Vital-sign integration means looking at the relationship between
measurements and the person. An initially fast rate can fall because
treatment helped or because the patient is becoming exhausted.

75
00:16:25,608 --> 00:16:37,440
In this original trend, the rate falls to eighteen but breaths become
barely visible, the person becomes drowsy and pressure falls. Those
findings do not describe recovery.

76
00:16:37,440 --> 00:16:51,192
Reassess and support airway, ventilation and circulation now. Confirm
the measurement and equipment without dismissing the patient's
deterioration as artifact merely because one number looks normal.

77
00:16:51,192 --> 00:17:06,240
Record time, device, oxygen flow, body position and associated
findings so that the receiver can interpret the trend. Good
documentation preserves the comparison rather than averaging away its
most important change.

78
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:46,512
Read the May 2026 BCEHS CPAP card as paired columns, not equal
numbers. Oxygen flows of 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10 L/min correspond on that
card to pressures of 5, 6, 7.5, 10, 12.5 and 15 cmH2O. Its treatment
cap remains 10 cmH2O: in the displayed baseline table, that is 8
L/min, not 10 L/min. Check the actual pressure gauge and device
configuration; this is a named service-card table, not a universal
equation for every CPAP system.

79
00:17:46,512 --> 00:18:26,256
Connect the ports by function on the May 2026 card: red is
supplemental oxygen, green is the nebulizer-tubing connection, and
black ports are marked for no prehospital use. The pressure gauge
measures pressure, not oxygen concentration. The card starts at 5
L/min and says an attached T-piece nebulizer configuration needs 7
L/min extra flow; the prose instead says 7-8. Preserve that difference
and follow the trained configuration while monitoring pressure, rather
than adding flow indiscriminately or treating an auxiliary connection
as the baseline pressure circuit.

80
00:18:26,256 --> 00:19:02,256
A nasal medicine is absorbed through nasal mucosa; the patient does
not need to inhale it into the lungs. J12 still says intranasal uptake
requires ongoing respirations, but the manufacturer explicitly states
that a person need not be breathing for its sealed NARCAN spray to
work. Do not turn that source wording into a reason to withhold an
otherwise indicated authorized nasal product in apnea. Continue
effective ventilation in parallel; product-specific instructions and
current service authority still govern route and dosing.

81
00:19:02,256 --> 00:19:36,696
PR11 limits delivered volume to 1 mL per nostril; if the volume is too
large, reconsider the route rather than pushing excess fluid into the
nose. A sealed single-dose nasal spray is used according to its own
instructions: do not open, dilute, prime, divide or add atomizer dead
space merely because a syringe technique was taught nearby. Reassess
the clinical effect and document product, amount, route, time and
response; a route change does not change the intended patient
endpoint.

82
00:19:36,696 --> 00:20:20,976
The literal page-371 boundary introduces button batteries, although
the school names J12 rather than J13. A battery lodged in the
esophagus can cause serious injury without obvious symptoms. Larger
20-25-mm lithium cells and young children are prominent source risk
contexts, but a smaller cell or quiet appearance cannot rule out
danger. Ask about missing batteries/devices and ingestion timing;
nausea, pain, cough, feeding difficulty or unusual noisy breathing may
be nonspecific clues. Assess the airway promptly, do not induce
vomiting and do not delay emergency-department conveyance to find
honey.

83
00:20:20,976 --> 00:21:00,720
Battery chemistry connects this endpoint to the assigned A&P chapter.
Tissue fluid completes an electrical circuit, producing hydroxide ions
and an alkaline burn, especially near the negative pole. The J13
opening incorrectly uses radical/coagulative-necrosis wording; its own
later body instead describes liquefactive necrosis, consistent with an
alkaline injury. This is not simply pressure from a coin-shaped
object. Honey is only a conditional measure under the complete
appropriate pathway, never a substitute for urgent removal or a
blanket oral treatment for an unsafe airway.

84
00:21:00,720 --> 00:21:13,248
An adult with wheeze becomes fatigued and has a history of beta-
blocker use. A medication name alone cannot tell you whether
ventilation is adequate or whether the airway diagnosis is complete.

85
00:21:13,248 --> 00:21:26,760
Explain why the actual bronchospasm findings support the selected
treatment, how the device delivers it and what effect you expect from
the receptor mechanism. Check the real contraindications and prior
exposure.

86
00:21:26,760 --> 00:21:39,936
Observe air entry, work and speech as well as pulse and oxygenation.
Consider whether the patient can actually use the interface; counting
sprays is not a substitute for delivered treatment.

87
00:21:39,936 --> 00:21:53,400
If breathing becomes ineffective, support it while reconsidering the
cause and obtaining help. Chemistry and receptor knowledge should
improve the decision, not distract you from a patient who needs a
breath.

88
00:21:53,400 --> 00:22:06,408
First define amount, concentration, ion, buffer, agonist and
antagonist without looking. Then give one example of how confusing two
terms could cause a clinical error.

89
00:22:06,408 --> 00:22:18,312
Trace both autonomic pathways and explain why acetylcholine is not
always parasympathetic. Predict an important effect and adverse effect
for the respiratory drugs assigned this week.

90
00:22:18,312 --> 00:22:31,896
Explain why oxygen saturation can improve while ventilation fails, why
high blood pressure does not prove good tissue flow and why an
indication-specific route can override a generic fastest-route
ranking.

91
00:22:31,896 --> 00:22:46,368
Finally use the reading index to review the detailed book sections,
then answer the original practice before opening its reasoning. Keep
unresolved school pages and activities on the coverage list instead of
marking them done by memory.
